{
  "$comment": "THE FIRST SHIPPED BUILDING THAT REACHES THE TOP OF THE SPEED CATALOGUE, and the densest group in the set: 106 cars over six banks against burj-class-reference's 57 over six. GitHub issue #424. NAMED IN THE REPOSITORY'S CONVENTION, burj-class-reference's: the real Shanghai Tower is the reference and the figures in this file are the project's own. PROVENANCE, per the product owner's 2026-09-08 standing ruling on governed data/ values: EVERY AUTHORED FIGURE BELOW IS AN AGENT'S PROPOSAL, drafted for the owner to accept, tighten or reject, and NOT YET APPROVED. The line the ruling asks for is drawn field by field. MEASURED (cited, and both citations are already in this repository): the shuttle's 20.5 m/s, which is elevator-specs.json's own realWorldAnchors row for this machine and the maximum of its ultra-high-speed class; and cabinPressurised, from the airPressure block's cited Al-Kodmany (Buildings 2015, 5(3), 1070-1104, s 3.1.4) note that this tower fits a pneumatic system against the air-pressure problem. The lift count, 106, is the one figure the prompting article states that this file uses, and it is used as a COUNT rather than as a citation -- how the 106 split between shuttles, locals and service is nowhere published and is chosen below. CHOSEN (an agent's proposals, none of them cited, all awaiting the owner): 128 floors above ground on a 4.5 m office storey and a 4.2 m hotel storey, putting the observation deck at 527.7 m and the crown's plant at 568.2 m under a 632 m architectural top this model does not carry; four sky lobbies at 21, 45, 69 and 93 and a hotel above the fourth; the populations (88 an office floor, 30 a hotel floor, 30 on the observation deck, 8,612 in all, which is roughly half what the real tower is reported to carry and is a floor-plate assumption rather than a citation); the split of the 106 cars (14 shuttles, four local banks of 20, a hotel bank of 12); and every car's class, rating and door. NOTHING HERE IS TAKEN FROM THE PROMPTING ARTICLE'S SPEED FIGURE: it says 5 m/s in the same paragraph as 'the 119th floor in under 55 seconds', which is self-refuting, and GitHub issue #424 says in terms that the article is the prompt and not the citation. WHAT THE DISTINGUISHING FEATURE BUYS, MEASURED ON THE LEGS RATHER THAN ASSERTED: packages/core/src/sim/directionalSpeedSeam.test.ts runs this tower as shipped and again with the shuttle rated at 10.0 m/s -- the fastest any shipped car was before this file -- and requires the legs to differ. A rating no run can spend is the shape of the eleven dead seams CLAUDE.md opens with, and CLAUDE.md's own 'short hops never reach rated speed' rule makes that a live risk at the top of the catalogue rather than a formality: at this class's 1.2 m/s2 and 1.2 m/s3 an s-curve needs about 185.4 m to reach 20.5 m/s and the same again to stop, so a leg shorter than about 370.7 m never touches the plate figure. THIS SHUTTLE MAKES EXACTLY ONE HOP THAT DOES -- G to sky lobby 4, 418.5 m -- and the other three (94.5 m, 202.5 m, 310.5 m) are acceleration-limited. That is the measurement #424 asks for, and it is in the seam test rather than in this sentence. WHAT THE CLOSED FORM SAYS: see packages/experiments/src/oracle/remainingBuildings.test.ts, which records this tower's verdict as a run rather than as a claim.",
